I attempted live prior-art search via three external tools; all were permission-denied in this session. Per the rule that a KILL collision must be verified in ≥2 independent sources, I therefore ground the assessment in the cited literature corpus and my own knowledge of it, and explicitly decline to assert any fatal collision I cannot verify live. That constraint itself caps my vote below GREEN.

```
ROLE: novelty_killer  VOTE: yellow

CLOSEST_PRIOR_WORK:
- {cite: "Duverger's Law / spoiler effect in plurality voting (social choice)", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duverger%27s_law", why_close: "This IS the mechanism. Under plurality, fragmenting one faction's votes across near-equivalent options lets a more concentrated faction win even with less total mass. The claim's 'errors-fragmented => merging consolidates wrong mass => hurts' is the spoiler effect applied to the incorrect class. The packet addresses CJT (Condorcet1785) and 'plurality theory' generically but NEVER names the spoiler/vote-splitting effect, which is the true mechanistic incumbent — not CJT."}
- {cite: "Bulian et al. 2022, 'Tomayto, Tomahto: Beyond Token-level Answer Equivalence for QA' (BEM)", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.07654", why_close: "Directly studies answer-equivalence canonicalization with an explicit precision/recall tradeoff vs exact-match; the 'recall of a canonicalizer' construct is theirs. They show exact-match under-credits correct answers (the phi>>0 'correct-fragmented' regime). Not cited; mandatory."}
- {cite: "Kuhn et al. 2023 'Semantic Uncertainty' / Farquhar et al. 2024 (semantic entropy, Nature)", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.09664", why_close: "Clusters semantically-equivalent generations before aggregating — i.e., a variable-granularity equivalence/merge stage over free-form outputs. Establishes that clustering granularity over generations is a tunable knob; the present claim is the maj@k-accuracy specialization of the same operation."}
- {cite: "Wang et al. 2022 'Self-Consistency' (2203.11171)", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.11171", why_close: "The monotone 'merge-more=better' baseline the claim contradicts — but it assumes pre-canonicalized atoms, so it does not actually occupy the variable-recall knob; correctly positioned as foil, not collision."}
- {cite: "Biderman et al. 2024 'Lessons from the Trenches on Reproducible Evaluation' (2405.14782); HF Open-LLM-Leaderboard MMLU parsing-sensitivity reports; Minerva (Lewkowycz 2022) SymPy normalization (2206.14858)", url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.14782", why_close: "Document that answer-extraction/parsing differences shift reported scores and rankings — the real-world version of the inversion claim. Establishes the phenomenon exists; weakens the inversion's novelty as a *surprise* (though not as a controlled measurement)."}

NAMING_VS_MECHANISM: Mostly NAMING/FRAMING novelty, thin mechanism delta. The core mechanism — that consolidating a fragmented faction under plurality can flip the winner — is the textbook spoiler/vote-splitting effect; the 'asymmetric sign governed by fragmentation asymmetry phi' is that effect parameterized by an entropy difference (phi = H_C - H_W) and applied symmetrically to the correct vs incorrect partitions, with all curves converging to the p_C plurality baseline at full recall (a known plurality limit, not a new result). The genuinely unoccupied contribution is the COMBINATION: framing the answer-equivalence canonicalizer's *recall* as a negative-capable causal eval knob, isolating it from precision (the precision-control is the most novel/valuable part), and proposing leaderboard inversion via grader-swap. That combination appears unoccupied in the LLM-eval methodology literature — but it is application novelty, not a new mechanism.

FATAL_COLLISIONS: None VERIFIED to the ≥2-independent-source bar (live search was permission-blocked, so I will not assert one). NON-fatal but load-bearing concern: the SECONDARY 'ranking inversion' adds little novel evidence — it is constructed by design from the spoiler effect over a hand-tuned param region (a_pc/b_pc retuned after the first attempt failed to invert), so it demonstrates the social-choice corollary, not an empirical discovery. Its novelty lives entirely in the L1 real-grader demonstration, which does not yet exist.

MANDATORY_RELATED_WORK:
- Spoiler effect / Duverger's law / vote-splitting (social choice) — the actual mechanistic incumbent; MUST be cited and explicitly differentiated, not just CJT.
- Bulian et al. 2022 (BEM, answer equivalence precision/recall) — the canonicalizer-recall construct.
- Kuhn 2023 / Farquhar 2024 (semantic entropy/clustering granularity over generations).
- Biderman et al. 2024 (2405.14782) + Minerva SymPy normalization (2206.14858) + HF leaderboard parsing-sensitivity reports — prior evidence that grader/extraction differences move rankings.
- Universal/Semantic Self-Consistency (Chen et al. 2023, 2311.17311) — free-form SC aggregation with an equivalence stage.

RATIONALE: Not GREEN: the mechanism is a known social-choice effect (spoiler/vote-splitting; Duverger) re-skinned as a 'canonicalizer recall knob,' and the closest mechanistic prior work (spoiler effect) is not even cited — only the weaker CJT incumbent is addressed. The answer-equivalence recall/precision construct is pre-existing (Bulian 2022), and grader-induced ranking shifts are documented (Biderman 2024, Minerva, HF leaderboard reports). Not RED: I cannot verify a fatal exact collision to the ≥2-source bar (and could not run live search), and the SPECIFIC unoccupied combination — recall isolated from precision as a sign-flipping causal eval knob, with the q-attenuation honestly scoped — is a legitimate, modest methodology contribution if (i) the spoiler/answer-equivalence/parsing-sensitivity literature is cited and differentiated, and (ii) L1 produces a REAL grader-swap leaderboard inversion (the constructed L0 one is a corollary, not evidence of novelty). YELLOW: real but thin, application-not-mechanism novelty, conditional on mandatory citations and a real-world L1 demonstration.
